<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Humphrey is a 65-year-old man with bladder cancer treated by radical cystoprostatectomy with an ileal conduit five months ago, who asked for a same-day visit. Two days ago he developed chills and an ache in the right flank, and last night his temperature at home was 38.6 °C. The urine in his bag has looked cloudier and smelled stronger, and there has been less of it, because he has been drinking less on busy days. He feels tired and has a poor appetite, but he has had no vomiting, no confusion, and no pain at the stoma. The stoma looks the same to him, and the appliance has not leaked since it was refitted. He has not had an antibiotic in the past year. He took acetaminophen last night and no other medicines. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Right flank</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Dull ache with chills</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; still eating and drinking, but less</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant, with fever in the evenings</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Long working days with reduced fluid intake; ileal conduit five months</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Acetaminophen eases the ache and fever for a few hours</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Cloudy, strong-smelling urine and lower output. Denies vomiting, confusion, stoma pain, or bleeding from the stoma</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

